WEBVTT

00:00:00.286 --> 00:00:02.161
Mia, the classroom lights are off.

00:00:02.442 --> 00:00:05.342
Can your eyes send out little
searchlights to find the book?

00:00:06.077 --> 00:00:06.712
I wish.

00:00:07.020 --> 00:00:11.908
I would never lose a sock again, but light
must enter my eyes, not shoot out of them.

00:00:12.709 --> 00:00:13.260
Correct.

00:00:13.597 --> 00:00:17.147
A luminous object, such as
a lamp, emits visible light.

00:00:17.528 --> 00:00:19.993
We see it when some of
that light enters our eyes.

00:00:20.501 --> 00:00:24.880
A non-luminous object, such as this
book, is seen by reflected light.

00:00:25.625 --> 00:00:26.927
Lamp to book to eye.

00:00:27.512 --> 00:00:31.809
The moon is non-luminous too because
we usually see reflected sunlight.

00:00:32.549 --> 00:00:33.323
Exactly.

00:00:33.626 --> 00:00:37.157
In a uniform medium, we model
light traveling in straight lines.

00:00:37.520 --> 00:00:41.059
A ray is a line showing
direction, not a physical string.

00:00:41.553 --> 00:00:43.610
Put arrowheads on your ray diagrams.

00:00:44.389 --> 00:00:48.448
Light travels around 300 million
meters per second in a vacuum.

00:00:48.963 --> 00:00:52.290
It is slightly slower in air
and slower still in glass.

00:00:53.068 --> 00:00:53.465
Good.

00:00:53.803 --> 00:00:57.660
Never stare at the sun or direct
lasers into anyone's eyes.

00:00:58.069 --> 00:01:02.659
Our diagrams and teacher-supervised
low-power ray box demonstrations let

00:01:02.659 --> 00:01:04.592
us learn without risking eyesight

00:01:05.021 --> 00:01:08.430
Clear glass, frosted
glass, and an opaque card.

00:01:08.920 --> 00:01:11.039
Describe what each does to visible light.

00:01:11.839 --> 00:01:15.244
Transparent glass transmits light
so we can see clearly through it.

00:01:15.615 --> 00:01:18.684
Translucent frosted glass
transmits and scatters light.

00:01:19.104 --> 00:01:22.041
An opaque card does not transmit
visible light through it.

00:01:22.903 --> 00:01:23.474
Yes.

00:01:23.971 --> 00:01:26.277
Opaque does not necessarily mean black.

00:01:26.596 --> 00:01:29.368
An opaque white card
reflects plenty of light.

00:01:29.770 --> 00:01:33.090
These descriptions also depend on
the wavelength we are considering.

00:01:33.891 --> 00:01:38.349
A shadow forms where an opaque object
blocks light from reaching a surface.

00:01:39.121 --> 00:01:41.467
Keep a small light
source and screen fixed.

00:01:41.773 --> 00:01:43.794
Move the object closer to the source.

00:01:44.118 --> 00:01:46.569
What generally happens to
its shadow on the screen?

00:01:47.473 --> 00:01:51.229
It gets larger because the object
blocks a wider bundle of rays

00:01:51.241 --> 00:01:52.596
before they reach the screen.

00:01:53.046 --> 00:01:55.240
We can trace the edges
with straight lines.

00:01:55.948 --> 00:01:56.332
Good.

00:01:56.708 --> 00:01:58.971
With a small source, edges can be sharp.

00:01:59.337 --> 00:02:03.174
With an extended source, some regions
receive light from only part of it.

00:02:03.633 --> 00:02:05.740
The fully shaded region is the umbra.

00:02:06.046 --> 00:02:08.386
The partly shaded region is the penumbra.

00:02:09.171 --> 00:02:12.664
So fuzzy edges do not mean light
has forgotten to travel straight.

00:02:13.108 --> 00:02:15.995
Different parts of the source
illuminate different regions

00:02:16.349 --> 00:02:19.570
Here is a ray hitting a mirror
at 30 degrees to the normal.

00:02:20.185 --> 00:02:21.750
What is the angle of reflection?

00:02:22.510 --> 00:02:23.603
60 degrees?

00:02:24.308 --> 00:02:26.044
I measured from the mirror surface.

00:02:26.791 --> 00:02:29.427
I can hear the exam mark
packing its suitcase.

00:02:30.201 --> 00:02:31.399
Bring that mark back.

00:02:31.833 --> 00:02:35.467
The normal is an imaginary line
at 90 degrees to the surface.

00:02:35.799 --> 00:02:37.654
Measure both angles from the normal.

00:02:37.964 --> 00:02:42.039
The angle of reflection equals the
angle of incidence, 30 degrees here.

00:02:42.854 --> 00:02:47.010
Then the angle between the incident ray
and the mirror surface is 60 degrees,

00:02:47.275 --> 00:02:49.408
but that is not the angle of incidence.

00:02:50.235 --> 00:02:51.022
Exactly.

00:02:51.315 --> 00:02:54.879
Draw a ruler straight normal
and clearly labeled angles.

00:02:55.219 --> 00:02:59.074
On a smooth mirror, parallel rays
remain orderly after reflection.

00:02:59.414 --> 00:03:03.401
On a rough surface, different
tiny surface orientations scatter

00:03:03.402 --> 00:03:04.741
them in different directions.

00:03:05.516 --> 00:03:08.526
Does diffuse reflection
break the equal angles law?

00:03:09.192 --> 00:03:09.563
No.

00:03:09.973 --> 00:03:13.914
Each small region still follows it
relative to its own local normal.

00:03:14.248 --> 00:03:17.452
Diffuse reflection helps us see
an ordinary page from different

00:03:17.452 --> 00:03:20.492
directions rather than only
one special viewing position.

00:03:21.310 --> 00:03:24.325
Now, describe the image in a plane mirror.

00:03:25.049 --> 00:03:29.058
Upright, the same size, and the
same distance behind the mirror

00:03:29.095 --> 00:03:30.523
as the object is in front.

00:03:31.025 --> 00:03:34.611
It is virtual because rays only
appear to come from behind the mirror.

00:03:35.420 --> 00:03:36.111
Excellent.

00:03:36.713 --> 00:03:40.113
The actual rays do not meet there,
so you cannot catch that image

00:03:40.113 --> 00:03:41.394
on a screen behind the mirror.

00:03:41.982 --> 00:03:46.552
Backward ray extensions are dashed because
no real light follows those extensions.

00:03:47.345 --> 00:03:49.803
People say mirrors swap left and right.

00:03:50.162 --> 00:03:51.325
Is that the whole story?

00:03:52.027 --> 00:03:55.325
The underlying reversal is front
to back relative to the mirror.

00:03:55.971 --> 00:03:59.880
When we compare ourselves with the
image, it appears laterally inverted,

00:04:00.105 --> 00:04:01.849
which is the usual school description.

00:04:02.236 --> 00:04:06.069
The mirror does not physically exchange
the world's left and right directions

00:04:06.444 --> 00:04:08.620
Light enters glass from air at an angle.

00:04:08.828 --> 00:04:10.224
Its speed decreases.

00:04:10.734 --> 00:04:13.527
Does the ray bend towards
the normal or away from it?

00:04:14.343 --> 00:04:15.325
Towards the normal.

00:04:15.808 --> 00:04:20.017
Returning from glass to air, it speeds
up and bends away from the normal,

00:04:20.264 --> 00:04:22.451
provided a transmitted ray emerges.

00:04:23.257 --> 00:04:24.089
Precisely.

00:04:24.451 --> 00:04:27.264
Refraction involves a change
in speed between media.

00:04:27.631 --> 00:04:31.579
When entry is perpendicular to the
boundary along the normal, speed

00:04:31.579 --> 00:04:33.512
changes without a change in direction.

00:04:34.278 --> 00:04:38.730
So saying it always bends whenever
its speed changes would be too strong.

00:04:39.552 --> 00:04:40.080
Correct.
